Subject: windows.ui.xaml: FontFamily, package fonts, glyph fallback and lazily loaded dictionary entries

FontFamily is now a class (FontFamilyFactory.CreateInstanceWithName, XamlAutoFontFamily) and an inherited
property: a FontFamily set on an element (from markup, a style setter, or Control/TextBlock/ContentPresenter's
put_FontFamily) applies to its text and its children's, as in WinUI. <FontFamily> resources become FontFamily
objects, and generic.xaml has WinUI's ContentControlThemeFontFamily and SymbolThemeFontFamily.

A FontFamily source may name a font file in the package ("ms-appx:///Resources/Fonts/Icons.ttf#Icons"): the file
is loaded into a DirectWrite font set the first time it is named. A comma-separated list falls back glyph by
glyph: a text is laid out in the first family that has all its characters (or the first one installed), and
characters it lacks go to the first family of the list that has them. Private Use Area characters that none of
them has go to Segoe Fluent Icons or Segoe MDL2 Assets, if installed.

Compiled resource dictionaries can refer to entries further on (WinUI loads them lazily): a {StaticResource}
or {ThemeResource} lookup that finds nothing while a dictionary loads now loads the entry it asks for first.

Xbox Accessories draws its icons ("[]" boxes before) from its own XboxFluent2.ttf through these, and its styles'
font, size and margin setters now apply.

